llery/YqbWmFm</link><description><![CDATA[<div><img alt="" src="https://i.imgur.com/jFse644.png" style="max-width: 90%; height: auto;"></div><div><span></span></div><div>A federal judge used the Sunday of a holiday weekend to order Donald Trump’s taxpayer-funded TV ads off the air.

Trump told his budget director to find the money, and Homeland Security came up with $20 million that Congress had appropriated for border security. One spot was his 2024 campaign ad warning of a “final battle” against “globalists,” except it ended with “Paid for by the U.S. Government” instead of the call to “Join President Trump’s Fight for America.” Speaker Mike Johnson said the ads weren’t “a plea to vote for anybody.”

The administration told Judge Jennifer Rochon the ads were ending Sunday anyway, with $19 million of that $20 million already spent on airtime. She ruled that the challengers are likely to win and ordered every remaining slot canceled.

